WEBVTT

00:00:09.165 --> 00:00:12.321
We will protect a count that two workers update.

00:00:12.321 --> 00:00:15.214
Watch how both jobs use the same object, how

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:18.369
synchronized methods control access, and why the

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:21.065
caller waits before reporting the result.

00:00:59.507 --> 00:01:03.100
Shared mutable state means stored values that several

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.930
threads can read or change.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.523
Both jobs keep a reference to this one SharedCounter.

00:01:08.523 --> 00:01:12.183
Its intrinsic monitor is the lock associated with that

00:01:12.183 --> 00:01:12.657
object.

00:01:12.657 --> 00:01:16.250
A synchronized instance method acquires that monitor.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:19.843
Mutual exclusion means only one cooperating thread at

00:01:19.843 --> 00:01:23.232
a time enters methods protected by this same lock.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.836
An increment reads a value, computes a new value, and

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:27.740
writes it back.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.514
A data race involves conflicting accesses from

00:01:30.514 --> 00:01:33.709
different threads without the required ordering, with

00:01:33.709 --> 00:01:34.855
at least one write.

00:01:34.855 --> 00:01:37.809
Protecting the whole increment makes it an atomic

00:01:37.809 --> 00:01:40.944
state transition: these related steps cannot overlap

00:01:40.944 --> 00:01:43.959
with conflicting access that follows the same lock

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:44.261
rule.

00:01:44.261 --> 00:01:47.396
Synchronization visibility means a later acquisition

00:01:47.396 --> 00:01:50.350
of that monitor observes writes ordered before an

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.315
earlier release.

00:01:51.462 --> 00:01:54.091
Predict the total after both jobs finish.

00:01:54.091 --> 00:01:57.297
The first requests two thousand increments and the

00:01:57.297 --> 00:01:58.708
second three thousand.

00:01:58.708 --> 00:02:01.915
What would change if each job received a different

00:02:01.915 --> 00:02:02.876
counter object?

00:02:02.876 --> 00:02:06.019
Trace the shared references and both joins before

00:02:06.019 --> 00:02:06.532
running.

00:02:06.532 --> 00:02:07.237
Pause here.

00:02:13.257 --> 00:02:15.482
The result is Total five thousand.

00:02:15.482 --> 00:02:18.950
Both starts happen before either join, and both joins

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.651
complete before this read.

00:02:20.651 --> 00:02:23.792
The getter also follows the counter’s lock rule.

00:02:23.792 --> 00:02:26.998
This result does not reveal which worker finished

00:02:26.998 --> 00:02:30.074
first or whether using two workers made the job

00:02:30.074 --> 00:02:30.532
faster.

00:02:31.004 --> 00:02:33.918
A lost update means one write replaces another

00:02:33.918 --> 00:02:37.274
worker’s change because both computed from an earlier

00:02:37.274 --> 00:02:37.654
value.

00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:40.441
As a reasoning example only, two unprotected

00:02:40.441 --> 00:02:43.672
increments could both read zero and both write one.

00:02:43.672 --> 00:02:45.698
We did not run that unsafe case.

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:49.055
With different counter objects, each job would update

00:02:49.055 --> 00:02:51.906
its own count; their separate locks would not

00:02:51.906 --> 00:02:53.679
coordinate one shared count.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:56.765
For a shared seat count, why should checking for a

00:02:56.765 --> 00:02:59.978
remaining seat and decreasing the count happen in one

00:02:59.978 --> 00:03:01.372
synchronized operation?

00:03:01.372 --> 00:03:04.343
Explain what could go wrong if they were separate

00:03:04.343 --> 00:03:07.556
calls, and why every caller must follow the same lock

00:03:07.556 --> 00:03:07.859
rule.
